  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 AM EDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nWeak disturbance moving eastward across Lake Michigan this \nmorning with clouds and a few rain showers associated with it.\nDid add some light rain showers this morning over northern half\nof Berrien County in Michigan where a few showers may make it \nto the surface as it pushes through. Radar imagery does show\nsome returns especially over the lake but with the drier low\nlevels much of it is likely virga. Also, adjusted low\ntemperatures up this morning by a few degrees as WAA ahead of\nthis disturbance along with some moisture indicated by slightly\nhigher surface dew points. This and the addition of some clouds\nmoving through with the disturbance will keep temperatures a tad\nwarmer than originally expected this morning. Temperatures \ncurrently as of 2:30 AM EDT are still in the low 50s here at the\noffice. \n\nA cold front behind this disturbance will move through the area\nlater today and this will keep high temperatures a bit cooler\n(in the upper 60s) for northwestern areas while highs will warm\ninto the low 70s further south and east. Clearing skies will\nalso take place later today with the cooler and drier airmass\nmoving into the area behind the cool front. \n\nDry weather will then be expected through the remainder of the\nwork week into next weekend and a cooler start to the week with\nlows back into the 40s on Monday morning with highs then only\ngetting into the low to mid 60s. Then a reinforcing shot of\ndrier and cooler air arrives Monday night with temperatures\ndropping into the low 40s and even getting in some locations to\nthe upper 30s. Will still have to watch this carefully as\nclear skies and light winds will allow for good radiational \ncooling and if a bit drier air moves in would allow for a bit\ncolder temperatures perhaps into the mid 30s for some locations.\nStill would recommend if you have temperature sensitive plants\nor animals that you protect them Monday night into Tuesday \nmorning out of an abundance of caution. \n\nA northwesterly flow aloft envelopes the region by midweek\nbecoming more zonal late in the week with a high amplitude ridge\nbuilding eastward into the central CONUS by next weekend. This\nwill provide for a warming trend from Tuesday into next weekend\nwith highs by Saturday and Sunday getting into the mid to upper\n70s. Perhaps a few locations along the southern tier of our CWA\ncould see close to 80. The next chance of precipitation looks to\nbe late in the weekend with a southern stream disturbance from\nTexas moving northeastward into the region.",
